Open business models democratize manufacturing and create opportunities for distributed innovation, repair, and adaptation by making processes, designs, and organizational structures transparent and accessible. When companies open-source designs, processes, or manufacturing knowledge, they enable third-party innovation, repair services, and remanufacturing ecosystems that extend product lifecycles significantly. This radical transparency builds community engagement and trust while reducing barriers to participation in circular systems. From a design perspective, products intended for open ecosystems must be exceptionally well-documented and designed for modularity to enable diverse stakeholders to contribute meaningfully.